  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Birds crashing into Lake Natron get calcified into stone mummies. Photographers found perfectly preserved bats and songbirds lining the shoreline.",
        "visual_keywords": "foggy lake shore mist"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The hyper-reflective water acts like an optical trap, luring flying animals to crash straight into the chemical bath.",
        "visual_keywords": "birds flying over water"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So what turns soft feathers and flesh into solid rock? Water temperatures reach 140 degrees, with alkalinity rivaling pure ammonia.",
        "visual_keywords": "steaming volcanic water ripples"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Surrounding volcanoes leak massive amounts of natron, a sodium compound that rapidly coats drowned animals in a calcified mineral crust.",
        "visual_keywords": "microscopic mineral crystallization abstract"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It is the exact salt ancient Egyptians used for mummification, making this lake a natural factory of living statues.",
        "visual_keywords": "ancient desert ruins sunset"
      }
    ],
